trary! We are doing relatively well in international comparison!














If we expand the whole thing to a global perspective, the differences become even more striking - and at this point the article has been updated: The currently (2019) richest person on earth, Amazon boss Jeff Bezos, currently has around 131 billion dollars. (3) This means that as an individual he is even more than the gross national product (GNP) of the 46 (!) smallest, mostly poorest countries on earth with millions upon millions of inhabitants! Over 2,000 people are dollar billionaires and own the unimaginable sum of 8.7 trillion dollars (5), which is 67 times the GNP of the 46 poorest countries mentioned above or the GNP of 154 of the lowest countries. Only 36 richer nations on earth are not included (4). (It is worth knowing that in 2009, when this article was written, the peak values ​​were much lower: the richest individual had 68 billion dollars, almost 1000 billionaires with a total of 3.5 trillion dollars)

We shrug our shoulders at such figures, which is quite understandable; hardly anyone can even begin to imagine anything of such astronomical magnitude. If someone has a monthly income of 20,000 euros, for example, they are often exposed to a lot of envy and pressure to justify themselves. But if someone earns 20 or 200 million euros a month, for example, they are usually met with admiration, even though they are receiving 1,000 or 10,000 times the income that essentially has to be earned by other people. This also gives them the corresponding opportunities to shape things, but more often also to abuse them. The reason for this admiration is simply that we can no longer understand such figures. Nevertheless, we must do our best to understand these magnitudes, as these sums are real and must gradually be distributed more fairly if social tensions nationally and worldwide and increasing poverty are not to get out of hand. To do this, it must simply be brought to the attention of as many people as possible who are no longer prepared to accept these grievances in the long term, in accordance with the statement of the American car manufacturer Henry Ford (1863-1947): "If people understood how our monetary system works, we would have a revolution - tomorrow!"

So let's imagine that a billion dollars is the average monthly net income of every household member in a German working-class household of 680,000 people. (6) That is slightly more than the population of Frankfurt/Main, or approximately of Graz, Linz, Salzburg and Klagenfurt combined, or the three largest cities in Switzerland: Zurich, Geneva and Basel, also added together.

Let's go further: in 2005, a Luxembourger with an average income of just over 80,000 dollars (which of course is not evenly distributed) could enjoy a per capita income 750 times higher than a resident of Burundi, who had to make ends meet for a year on an incredible 107 dollars (7) - or simply couldn't manage it! Since income is not even evenly distributed in Burundi, the few richer people here also embellish the statistics; in concrete terms, that means that the majority of Burundians do not even have anywhere near 8 dollars a month to live on!


This brings us to the fact that the extremely unequal distribution of money has not only taken on unpleasant and unfair forms, but downright scandalous ones. In 2007, around 25,000 people died of starvation every day, that is, one person every 3 seconds, and a very large number of them were children!

This is not because there is no food left for them, but because they simply cannot afford it ! (8)



