The curse of money

The distribution problem alone leads many people to speak of the "curse of money". This assessment is reinforced by other problems of the monetary system, for example the downright enslavement of millions of people who invest the vast majority of their life energy in increasing their wealth and often become blind to the social needs of their fellow human beings. It is not for nothing that Jesus of Nazareth said: "It is easier for a rope to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God" (9). These internally enslaved people are faced with an even larger army of externally enslaved people who have become dependent due to a lack of capital and who have to offer their only economic asset, their work, at an inhumane price due to distortions in the monetary system!

Another major problem lies in the structure of the monetary system: as easy as it is to use on a daily basis, it becomes complicated in all its variations with countless special terms from banking, stocks and other investment opportunities. At least the "locust" problem of stock funds, especially some notorious private equity companies and hedge funds, has raised awareness of the problem among larger sections of society because it was reported in the media.

This refers to the current problem of capital-rich fund companies attacking otherwise healthy companies like swarms of locusts, literally draining them dry and then distributing the profits they make, often in the order of well over 20 percent per year, to their already rich investors, thereby further increasing their opportunities for abuse. What remains are destabilized companies that have to make mass layoffs or go bankrupt.

Nevertheless, the complexity of the system prevents almost every average citizen from dealing with the issue of the monetary system, although it is urgently necessary. Therefore, the aim of this GrailWorld series is to break through the tangle of complicated issues in an understandable way to the very simple core of the overall problem!

Another area of ​​the general public's lack of awareness of the problem is the rampant national debt. We usually accept it with a shrug of the shoulders as something that actually has nothing to do with us. But this assessment is misleading! We are the state! It is ourDebt! In concrete terms, Germany has an unimaginable debt burden of around 1.5 trillion euros, that is 1,500,000 million euros! Every German, from infants to the elderly, has 18,200 euros (10) in debt, for which the interest alone must be partially recovered in the form of ever-increasing taxes and levies. Only partially, because in order to avoid major social tensions, the rest is again "elegantly" regulated through further new debt. In Germany, for example, depending on the economic situation and thus tax revenue situation, new debt is currently "only" rising by 540 euros per second due to the good economic situation! (11) About a year and a half ago, the figure was an unbelievable 2,300 euros! (12)

It is also worth knowing that our political whitewashers understand a "balanced budget" to mean that, for once, no new debt is added to the mountain of old debt. Even this goal is almost never achieved! In Germany, for example, we would be truly debt-free after an incredible 125 years if we paid off one billion euros a month in real terms! It should only be mentioned in passing that this repayment would cost three to four times as much in interest, and at the beginning even 7.5 times (13). Repayment is therefore not even an option at the moment; on the contrary, debt is growing!

In Austria and Switzerland, per capita debt is almost identical to that in Germany. This is a very special achievement for Switzerland, as this peace-loving country has had the franc since 1852 (14), and a functioning economy even longer, while the Germans built up the same level of debt after the Second World War, since 1948 with the introduction of the DM.

In the USA, the situation is even more serious: national debt is around 9 trillion dollars, per capita debt is around 30,000 dollars, daily new debt is 1.45 billion dollars (15), which is roughly equivalent to the USA's daily military expenditure! The problem is made even worse by the fact that private consumer loans are extremely widespread. In addition to the national debt mentioned, countless households are in the red even further. Nevertheless, this country with the highest debt in the world is still considered the richest. -




