Happy through money?

Those who are chronically short of money are generally not to be envied, because a lot of energy and perseverance must be expended to find new sources of money, even for the bare necessities. In many cases, in addition to the work, the whole thing is accompanied by begging or submission to those who have to distribute the money.

But even those who are rich are generally not happy! As already mentioned, many people enslave themselves to the accumulation of money, far beyond their actual needs! They compare interest rates, study the ups and downs of the stock market, test their "courage" on lucrative but risky forms of investment, etc. In this way, their lives are ultimately exhausted with abstract numbers, which are of no use to them at the latest when they die, because, as we all know, not a single cent can be taken with them into the afterlife. What they take with them instead is the karma and the psychological imprint that may have been formed through the misuse of money, for example stinginess, hard-heartedness and greed, as well as guilt towards the people harmed by it and, last but not least, the missed opportunity to develop spiritually and mentally. Or they enjoy the power that comes with owning money, both as an individual and as a state. Of course, this also includes the great danger that misusing this power can bring and unfortunately too often does!

Or they live off the interest on their investments, the price gains or the dividends from their shares, which may seem quite princely on the outside, but essentially means they stop doing any meaningful activity, which inevitably leads to psychological numbness or, in the best case, to an existential crisis that demands a way out. The tabloids and the “reggae press” are full of examples of how people in “high society” get caught up in scandals, how they try to cover up their hopeless inner emptiness with excessive parties, frequent relationship changes or by taking drugs.

If you don’t want to take the term “happiness” to the point of absurdity, this alone would prove that money does not necessarily make you happy!

Of course there are counterexamples, people who really use their wealth responsibly and usefully for the benefit of many others, but unfortunately they are in the stark minority.

So it is not surprising that many level-headed people talk about the “curse of money” and wish to turn away from this sinister matter, this mysterious means of power and measure of value!


Alternatives to money

Let us therefore consider whether the human invention of money is necessary at all and whether it should be classified as beneficial or harmful by looking at the alternatives to money: This is actually very simple, because there are only two: self-sufficiency and the exchange of goods for goods or work for work - or a combination of both.

Self-sufficiency is the simplest method of avoiding dealing with money. You take care of satisfying your own needs and those of your loved ones. Water is provided by the nearest well, food is provided by the garden, a house and perhaps a stable for keeping animals can also be built with skill, etc. But from these few examples it is easy to imagine how it will continue: it quickly becomes time-consuming and complicated, and producing paper or window glass on your own, let alone a refrigerator or a television, is no longer conceivable in this way. With exclusive self-sufficiency, everything is inevitably at a low technical and cultural level. The coexistence of people with their different abilities suggests that they should be able to participate in a wider range of goods and services, at least through exchange. But even here the limits quickly become apparent, which can be summed up as follows: the search for suitable trading partners, often through barter, would always be time-consuming, agreement on the value and thus the number of the respective exchange goods would be difficult, bridging greater distances with the goods would not make much sense, etc. The result would be a great loss of time and a huge effort, which would often lead to total failure, particularly with perishable goods. A society based on the division of labour, even remotely similar to our usual structure, would in any case be completely out of the question on an exchange basis.

Now to money! In contrast, it is an ideal means of exchange, as it can be broken down into pieces at will and is therefore suitable as a "means of transporting value" in a form adapted to the value of the goods. The only requirement is that it must be generally recognised and accepted. This is why money used to have to be made of precious metals such as gold and silver, or at least be covered by a corresponding amount of this gold. But this was no longer sustainable in the long term and ultimately not necessary. Today, printed paper, banknotes and coins made of non-precious metals are sufficient.

The bartering rings that are becoming more popular today certainly have their advantages, and there is nothing wrong with striving for greater self-sufficiency. However, both in the form we are used to are based on a structure that could never have come into being in all its diversity without the possibilities of money!

For this reason, the monetary system has existed in all advanced cultures for thousands of years.

The question of whether money is a blessing or a curse can therefore be answered quite simply and clearly: it is a good and beneficial invention, as it reduces the effort required for the necessary exchange processes between people, be it goods or services, to the lowest possible level, leaving them plenty of time for other activities or amenities! It also prevents many foods from spoiling, which cannot always be exchanged through barter. Above all, however, it promotes economic and cultural diversity in society, which could not come about through other means.


The apparent dual nature of money

Nevertheless, as already described, the "curse" of money has also become obvious and raises the question of whether money itself has an opposing dual nature, i.e. a good and a bad side. So that along with the advantage one must inevitably accept the disadvantage, the dark side of money? So does the use of money inevitably bring with it over time all the phenomena that we can observe in the past and present: impoverishment of the weak, exploitation of nature and natural resources, inflation and excessive indebtedness, "overheating" and finally the collapse of economic and ecological cycles and systems? Since these unpleasant side effects of the use of money have repeatedly shown themselves on a small and large scale, nationally and internationally, historically and currently, it is logical to regard the regularly recurring economic catastrophes as inevitable. We usually judge based on our experiences, like: “It’s always been like this, so we have to accept it!”

But isn’t the regularity of these crises a serious indication that something is wrong with the system? That is indeed the case!

In addition, we humans, as spiritual beings, are like a magnifying glass according to the laws of nature.act which concentrates and thereby strengthens the forces flowing through it. This is how we have the concept of "currents of the times" and also the "pull of the masses". But we have undoubtedly found money to be far more attractive than average; it offers power, influence, well-being and convenience through its ability to be exchanged for goods and work at will. But this extremely strong preoccupation of millions or even billions of people with money has caused it to grow, through the "magnifying glass effect", to a power and significance that surpasses everything else. In general human attitudes today it is therefore unfortunately quite right that "money rules the world", not humanity, virtues or, of course, in the final analysis, the Creator.


When the system becomes ill

If any system develops an oversized power, we face the problem that the smallest error in it has very far-reaching consequences. If it is a major error, a natural impossibility as the basis of the monetary system, then the greatest devastation is programmed and unavoidable. And such an unnatural basis, a real cancer in the otherwise healthy monetary system, has actually existed since biblical times up to the present day: interest and compound interest!

Most people would probably disagree with this negative assessment, however, because interest generally enjoys an excellent reputation! In fact, it is usually even seen as a great benefactor; it obviously gives us an additional income, albeit in many cases only a modest one, for which we do not have to work. Almost everyone therefore views it with kindness and benevolence, and has no idea that it, and only it, is the greatest enemy of a healthy economic system and its surest gravedigger. It brings about the collapses that we are usually puzzled by, mathematically predictable in advance.

And not only we, but even most financial experts, right up to the top money guardians and finance ministers, have strangely failed to recognize the real cause, because otherwise effective countermeasures would have to be taken! Accordingly, today's increasingly desperate countermeasures, such as strict savings, large tax increases and attempts to induce ever greater economic growth, are bound to fail because they miss the real problem.

